// EXPECT-DIAG: E048
// EXPECT-FIX: replace 'io.printline' with 'io::printline'
// EXPECT-DIAG: 'io' is a module, not an instance
// Regression test: `.` is instance-member-access only -- a module-qualified
// function call MUST use `::` (io::printline(...)), never `.`
// (io.printline(...)).
//
// Before this fix, `.` silently worked as an undocumented ALIAS for `::`
// here: Resolver::visitMethodCall() had its own separate, fully-working
// resolution path (VariableExprAST::ResolvedModuleName) for a `.`-call
// whose base named a known module, threaded all the way through
// TypeChecker and Codegen -- `io.printline("x")` compiled and ran
// identically to `io::printline("x")`. This directly contradicted this
// language's stated design ("'.' is instance-member-only, '::' is the
// general namespace-resolution operator") despite the whole `::`
// generalization effort earlier this session. Found by direct user
// request to actually verify (not just assume) that '.' is rejected and
// '::' is accepted, and not both.

import io;

fn main(): int {
    io.printline("this should not compile");
    return 0;
}
